From 541d2b34130ff6ec38ddc61ae7122cb95c7b7b1d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Date: Tue, 1 Sep 2026 21:20:53 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] Add getNfwNotifications

Reports when something outside the platform - a carrier, the modem, or the GNSS
chip vendor - asks the device for its location, and whether it was given. The
GNSS stack raises these through IGnssVisibilityControl; nyx carries them as
nyx_gps_nfw_notification_t, and until now nothing surfaced them.

This is deliberately not folded into the network-initiated location flow. An NI
request asks the user to allow or refuse before the fact and expects an answer;
this reports what already happened and takes none. They are different events and
want different UI, so they get different methods.

Subscription only: the notifications arrive when the stack decides to raise one,
so there is nothing to return at call time and no handler to start. It also does
not require the GPS handler to be on, because the point of the notification is
to tell the user their location was accessed - which is most worth knowing when
they did not ask for it.

The callback arrives on the binder thread, so it hands off to the main loop
before touching the subscription list, the same hop the NMEA and status
callbacks make. Registration is separate from nyx_gps_init because it is not
part of the position flow, and a HAL without IGnssVisibilityControl - every 1.x
one - simply answers NYX_ERROR_NOT_IMPLEMENTED, which is logged and ignored.

Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [LuneOS specific]
